    2-2.1  Device (Serial Device Name)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is where you define your serial device name.
    Do NOT add a path, only the actual name!
    WARNING: It is case sensitive, has to be EXACTLY the same as the name
             on disk.


    2-2.2  Unit (Serial Device Unit Number)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is the actual unit number of the serial port for this node.
    Amiga internal serial port is device number 0.
    A2232 cards start at device number 2-up.


    2-2.3  Baud (Maximum DTE Baud Rate)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is your maximum DTE rate allowed by your modem to communicate
    with the computer.

    - A2232 Serial Cards can only use maximum of 19200.
    - Amiga Internal Serial Port would use a setting of 38400 or 57600.
    - Not much need to set internal serial port any higher than 57600.
    - All other serial cards can go 115200 or higher without a problem.


    2-2.4  Min Baud (Minimum Baud Rate Connect)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is the minimum baud rate a user is allowed to be at.  Anything
    slower than this specified baud rate will just display the too slow
    text in the text directory and kick the user off.


    2-2.5  Wait (Secs to wait for Connect)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Seconds the modem waits for a connect before resetting modem for a new
    caller.
    Recommended setting of: 35-45


    2-2.6  Rings (Number of Rings before Connect)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Number of rings before the modem picks up and gives carrier.  If you have
    problems with user's not being able to connect, reset this back to 0.


    2-2.7  Ser Buffer (Serial Buffer Size)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is where you set the serial buffer size to be used.
    The setting is in kilobytes, so a setting of 16 = 16k.
    Possible Settings: 1-255
    Default Setting: 16k


    2-2.8  R/W Buffer (Read/Write Buffer Size)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Currently not active.
    Possible Settings: 1-255


    2-2.9  PDelay (Modem Pause Delay)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Delay (In Ticks) for the modem pause command '~'
    Recommended setting of: 25


    2-2.10  CDelay (Modem Command Delay)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Delay (In Ticks) that is given between modem string command characters.
    Recommended setting of: 5


    2-2.11  Handshake
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Toggles Handshaking modes:
    1) RTS/CTS Handshaking (Default)
    2) XON/XOFF
    3) Both Active
    4) None Active
    Recommended setting of: RTS/CTS


    2-2.12  Bits Char
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Toggles Bits Char modes:
    1) 8 Bits (Default)
    2) 7 Bits
    Recommended setting of: 8 Bits


    2-2.13  Parity
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Toggles Parity modes:
    1) None (Default)
    2) Even
    3) Odd
    4) Mark
    5) Space
    Recommended setting of: None


    2-2.14  Stop Bits
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Toggles Stop Bits modes:
    1) 1 Bit (Default)
    2) 2 Bits
    Recommended setting of: 1 Bit


    2-2.15  Sleep Timeout (in minutes)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is the setting set to control the idle time of a user online.  If
    a user is sitting on the bbs and not hitting any key, this timer will
    activate an automatic kick off of the user if they don't hit a key
    in the specified amount of minutes.
    Recommended setting of: 5 (5 Minutes)


    2-2.16  Auto Load
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Whether you want the node to automaticly load when the server is loaded.
    Recommended setting of: Checked On


    2-2.17  Open Device
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Whether you want the node to open the serial device or if you want it to
    be a local node for sysop use only with no serial routines opened.
           Real Node Setting: Checked On
    Local Sysop Node Setting: Checked Off


    2-2.18  Front End Mode
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is used only if you are going to use a front end utility like
    TrapDoor to answer the phone and then have TSpawner transfer control
    of the call to the bbs.  Otherwise you will always have it set to
    Normal: BBS Answers Phone
    When setting this flag on, shared serial mode is turned on as well.


    2-2.19  Shared (Shared Serial)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    When this flag is turned on, other applications will be able to open
    up the same serial device.
    Recommended Setting of: Checked On


    2-2.20  DTR Hangup
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Whether to drop the DTR when hanging up.
    Recommended Setting of: Checked On


    2-2.21  Null Modem
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is used to define a node as a null modem node, hooked up to a null
    modem cable to another machine.  If you are not hooking up the node to
    a null modem cable, this option should be off.


    2-2.22  Rad Boggie
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is an "overdrive" option, and ONLY works on bauds 9600 and
    above.  Users will get slightly better CPS rates with this on.
    If you are running Tempest with a 2400 baud modem, then this may
    cause errors if selected.
    Checking this box will also Change Handshaking to CTS/RTS, Change 
    Parity to None and set Char/Bits to 8.  These settings are going
    to be the defaults as well so nothing will probably be changed.

    Recommended setting of: Yes (9600+ Baud Modem)
    Recommended setting of: No  (below 9600+ Baud Modem)


    2-2.23  2232 Flush (Serial Flush)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is only active when you have a node on a 2232 Serial Card Port.
    Recommended setting of: Checked Off, Unless you are using 2232 Card.


    2-2.24  Telnet
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This flag should only be turned on if the node is a telnet node.
    To setup a telnet node you must use the telnetd.device.



